course: dictionary
lesson: 5875
title: Mange and mangy
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Mange is a noun. It is a skin disease in animals such as dogs and foxes, caused by tiny mites that burrow into the skin. It makes the fur fall out and the skin itchy and sore.
explain: «Foxes in towns often have mange, which is why some look bald and thin.» The clues bald and thin tell you the fox is ill, not just untidy. «Vets can treat it with medicine.» Vets are animal doctors, so mange is an illness they can cure.
explain: The adjective mangy means suffering from mange. In everyday talk it also means shabby or scruffy. «A mangy old coat» is not an animal, so here mangy means worn out and scruffy. Always check the context to see which meaning fits.
fact: Mange is uncountable, so we say "some mange" or "the mange", not "a mange".
fact: Mites are tiny creatures, far too small to see easily, that live in or on skin.
fact: «Mangy» means having mange, or looking shabby and scruffy.
fact: «Je ne mange pas» is French and means "I do not eat". Here mange is a verb, the form of manger, so it is a different word that only looks the same.
fact: Vets are the doctors who treat sick animals, and they can give medicine for mange.
exercise: «The fox looked bald and thin, and its skin was sore and itchy.» What is the most likely cause? | Too much sunshine | Running too fast | A new winter coat | *Mange
exercise: What does mange mean? | A fast way of running | A sweet fruit eaten in hot countries | *A skin disease in animals, caused by tiny mites | A kind of medicine for people
exercise: «a mangy old coat» What does mangy mean here? | Very wet and cold | Brand new and shiny | Made of soft silk | *Shabby and scruffy
test: Which word is the adjective, meaning shabby or suffering from mange? | mange | mangle | *mangy | manger
test: «Tiny mites burrow into the skin.» What causes mange? | *Tiny mites | Cold weather | Lack of sleep | Too much food
exam: Which sentence uses mange as a noun? | «They mange their coats every week.» | «The dog will mange the bone tomorrow.» | «The fox is mangy and runs quickly.» | *«The vet treated the mange on the dog.»
